<p>
If you&rsquo;ve been around Irreal for a while, you know that I&rsquo;ve always been conflicted about ad blockers. On the one hand, I recognize that content providers have to make money some how and that the only reasonably way in today&rsquo;s Internet is through ads. Because of that, I&rsquo;ve always said that I&rsquo;m willing to see ads just as I was in the old days when we consumed content from magazines and newspapers.
</p>
<p>
My only objection was that the ads had become surveillance mechanisms that would track you wherever you went on the Internet. Enough is enough and I finally declared that I was going to block ads and would stop when the advertisers stopped tracking me. In today&rsquo;s world, that means never.
</p>
<p>
Happily, my use of <a href="https://www.magiclasso.co/">Magic Lasso</a> removed all the trackers and much more besides. As a result, I could still surf the Web and not have to worry about being tracked. I was still waiting for the advertisers to stop tracking so I could turn off the add blocker.
</p>
<p>
Sadly, I have to upgrade that policy. As I&rsquo;ve written before, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13028">I use Xwidgets</a> to display my Emails and Elfeed entries in HTML. The thing about Xwidgets in Emacs is that it doesn&rsquo;t run Magic Lasso or any other ad blocker so I get to see what the Web is like without ad blockers.
</p>
<p>
It&rsquo;s not pretty. There are multiple popups that cover the content, videos that play—and loudly—without permission and many other affronts to one&rsquo;s sensibilities. As Cory Doctorow is fond of saying, it&rsquo;s the enshittificaiton of the Web.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;m not against sites showing us ads in a way similar to what magazines and newspapers have traditionally done but if you want your ad to pop up and cover the content I&rsquo;m trying to read or you want to play some video without invitation, I&rsquo;m going to continue blocking your ads until you stop.</p>
